WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.768 --> 00:00:08.752
The story of the Suez Canal is a story of determination.

2
00:00:09.473 --> 00:00:15.916
It started as a simple idea, turned into a grand dream, and finally became a reality.

3
00:00:16.597 --> 00:00:21.539
The man behind this dream was a French diplomat named Ferdinand de Lesseps.

4
00:00:23.601 --> 00:00:29.724
Born in France in 1805, Ferdinand was a bright, young man with a promising future.

5
00:00:30.504 --> 00:00:34.648
His father was a diplomat who worked in many countries around the world.

6
00:00:35.469 --> 00:00:42.396
Ferdinand studied to become a diplomat like his father, and in 1832 he was sent to Egypt to

7
00:00:42.456 --> 00:00:46.419
take a diplomatic job as the French consul at Alexandria.

8
00:00:47.561 --> 00:00:53.526
Upon his arrival in the ancient city, however, Ferdinand had to stay in a hospital because someone from

9
00:00:53.566 --> 00:00:55.088
his ship had fallen sick.

10
00:00:55.872 --> 00:01:01.636
While he and others from the ship waited to learn if they were sick too, Ferdinand grew restless

11
00:01:02.156 --> 00:01:03.637
and picked up a book to read.

12
00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:10.881
The book was a report written by a French engineer, and reading it inspired a dream that Ferdinand

13
00:01:10.961 --> 00:01:13.203
pursued for the rest of his life.

14
00:01:15.024 --> 00:01:20.307
The report was about building a waterway between the Mediterranean and Red seas.

15
00:01:20.987 --> 00:01:27.889
At that time ships sailing between Europe and Asia had to travel around the entire continent of Africa.

16
00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:34.495
If built, this new route would greatly shorten the trip between Europe and the Far East.

17
00:01:36.096 --> 00:01:42.722
The report stated that building a canal from the Mediterranean to the Red Sea was impossible because of

18
00:01:42.762 --> 00:01:45.205
the different water levels of the two seas.

19
00:01:46.085 --> 00:01:48.848
But this conclusion did not discourage Ferdinand.

20
00:01:49.564 --> 00:01:55.868
Instead, as soon as the young consul was released from the hospital, he began researching the narrow piece

21
00:01:55.909 --> 00:02:00.512
of land, called the Isthmus of Suez, that separated the two seas.

22
00:02:02.053 --> 00:02:07.877
Ferdinand discovered that the pharaohs of Ancient Egypt had built waterways to connect the Nile River to the

23
00:02:07.957 --> 00:02:13.721
Red Sea to have access to the rich copper mines and spice lands of East Africa.

24
00:02:14.661 --> 00:02:20.861
Unfortunately, this was thousands of years in the past, and those waterways were long lost.

25
00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:27.402
But now that he knew a similar canal had once been built, Ferdinand was more determined than ever

26
00:02:27.803 --> 00:02:29.203
to pursue his goal.

27
00:02:31.464 --> 00:02:35.084
Ferdinand de Lesseps' duties as the French consul were demanding.

28
00:02:35.805 --> 00:02:42.667
He traveled frequently between Alexandria and Cairo, and had little time for his beloved Suez Canal project.

29
00:02:43.727 --> 00:02:49.273
It was also difficult to get the ruler of Egypt to support his plans, even though Ferdinand was

30
00:02:49.333 --> 00:02:53.114
teaching French and horseback riding to the ruler's grandson.

31
00:02:54.355 --> 00:02:56.656
Still Ferdinand did not give up.

32
00:02:57.116 --> 00:03:02.598
He continued to study the height of the land and the water levels along possible routes for the

33
00:03:02.638 --> 00:03:11.081
canal. In 1837 Ferdinand was transferred from Egypt back to Europe to take up other diplomatic posts.

34
00:03:11.942 --> 00:03:17.344
He was sad to leave, and it was twenty years before he saw his beloved Egypt again.

35
00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:25.044
During those years, as his diplomatic responsibilities increased, his dream of the canal began to fade.

36
00:03:27.206 --> 00:03:33.529
In 1849 Ferdinand retired and went to live in a villa on the outskirts of Paris.

37
00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:39.133
It seemed to him that his great dream belonged to a part of his life that was over,

38
00:03:39.633 --> 00:03:41.654
but things were about to change.

39
00:03:42.675 --> 00:03:48.842
One morning in 1854, Ferdinand was reading the newspaper when a headline caught his eye.

40
00:03:50.323 --> 00:03:56.847
He read that Abbas Pasha, the ruler of Egypt, was dead and that Said Pasha had inherited the

41
00:03:56.867 --> 00:04:02.690
position. Said Pasha was the boy Ferdinand had taught back in Alexandria.

42
00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:08.813
The young prince was now a grown man who remembered the retired French consul fondly.

43
00:04:09.533 --> 00:04:14.136
Ferdinand knew that the new ruler of Egypt would support him in building the canal.

44
00:04:15.144 --> 00:04:17.706
His dream was coming to life once again.

45
00:04:18.567 --> 00:04:25.272
When Said invited Ferdinand to come to Egypt to celebrate his new position, Ferdinand promptly agreed.

46
00:04:26.293 --> 00:04:31.737
In the time since Ferdinand's first posting to Egypt, the reports about the different water levels of the

47
00:04:31.777 --> 00:04:35.060
Mediterranean and Red seas had been proved wrong.

48
00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.303
Now the only obstacle was finding the money to build the canal.

49
00:04:40.424 --> 00:04:45.484
Almost everyone thought Ferdinand was out of his mind to dig a canal in the desert, and he

50
00:04:45.544 --> 00:04:48.045
had a hard time finding financial supporters.

51
00:04:48.725 --> 00:04:53.547
But his diplomatic charm, optimism, and belief in the project prevailed.

52
00:04:54.367 --> 00:04:58.928
Ferdinand traveled between Europe and Egypt, gathering support for his project.

53
00:05:01.649 --> 00:05:07.371
It took him five years, but he was able to find supporters in France and Egypt who bought

54
00:05:07.491 --> 00:05:10.832
shares in his newly-formed Suez Canal Company.

55
00:05:11.528 --> 00:05:19.175
In 1859 he finally had enough funds to start digging; construction began in April of that year.

56
00:05:20.036 --> 00:05:25.381
Once construction started, it took ten years of hard labor to complete the canal.

57
00:05:27.162 --> 00:05:30.886
Said Pasha supplied Ferdinand with the many laborers he needed.

58
00:05:31.566 --> 00:05:38.448
A total of one and a half million laborers worked on the canal, mostly by hand, digging in

59
00:05:38.488 --> 00:05:43.910
the sandy soil in extremely hot weather with almost no machinery to aid them.

60
00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:54.433
Finally, after much hardship and the death of many laborers, the Suez Canal was completed in 1869.

61
00:05:55.053 --> 00:05:59.574
The day the Suez Canal officially opened, there was a grand celebration.

62
00:06:00.454 --> 00:06:06.476
Important people from all over the world came to Egypt, including Empress Eugenie of France.

63
00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:12.965
It had taken Ferdinand over forty years of hard work, but his dream had been realized.

64
00:06:13.946 --> 00:06:16.849
Europe and the Far East were finally connected.

65
00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:27.438
The Suez Canal, one of the longest in the world, is 166 kilometers long, 11 meters deep, and

66
00:06:27.458 --> 00:06:28.919
60 meters wide.

67
00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:34.736
It can accommodate any ship except the largest supertankers.

68
00:06:34.756 --> 00:06:40.298
Each year thousands of ships from almost every country in the world travel through the canal.

69
00:06:41.318 --> 00:06:47.581
In fact, 14 percent of all the goods traded worldwide pass through this waterway.

70
00:06:48.321 --> 00:06:54.423
Ferdinand's dream to connect the world took him on a long and difficult journey, but in the end,

71
00:06:54.923 --> 00:06:57.664
the determined French diplomat succeeded.
